{
  "status": "success",
  "user_request": "I mention breathalyzers in my essay at https://docs.google.com/document/d/1U3EYa9gum02TSImK8Z8xwWnfu6jLFjFL7v9onqT42lw/export?format=txt. What exactly is a breathalyzer and how does it work?",
  "response": "A breathalyzer is a device that measures alcohol in the air a person exhales. It does not directly measure blood alcohol content (BAC); instead, it estimates BAC from the breath-alcohol level. After alcohol enters the bloodstream, some passes into the air in the lungs and is exhaled. The device analyzes a breath sample and uses the relationship between breath alcohol and blood alcohol—often represented by a partition ratio of roughly 2,100:1—to calculate an estimated BAC. Different devices use different sensors: some use a fuel cell, in which alcohol produces an electrical signal; others use infrared light to detect alcohol molecules or a chemical reaction that changes color. In short, a breathalyzer tests alcohol in breath and converts that measurement into an estimate of alcohol in blood. Sources: [McGill University](https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/article/did-you-know/did-you-know-breathalyzers-dont-directly-measure-your-blood-alcohol-concentration), [Medical News Today](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/breathalyzer-test), and [UCSF Health](https://www.ucsfhealth.org/care/medical-tests/breath-alcohol-test)."
}

