{
  "status": "success",
  "user_request": "I mention breathalyzers in my essay at https://docs.google.com/document/d/1U3EYa9gum02TSImK8Z8xwWnfu6jLFjFL7v9onqT42lw/export?format=txt. What exactly is a breathalyzer and how does it work?",
  "response": "A breathalyzer is a device that tests a person’s exhaled breath for alcohol. “Breathalyzer” began as a brand name, but it is commonly used to refer to breath-alcohol testing devices generally. After alcohol enters the bloodstream, some passes from blood into the air in the lungs. The person blows into the device, which tests a breath sample and uses its alcohol reading to estimate blood alcohol concentration (BAC). It estimates BAC rather than directly measuring alcohol in blood; one commonly used conversion assumes about 2,100 mL of breath corresponds to the alcohol in 1 mL of blood.\n\nDifferent devices detect alcohol in different ways. Fuel-cell models produce an electrical current through a chemical reaction with alcohol; infrared models detect how alcohol absorbs infrared light; and semiconductor models detect changes in a sensor’s electrical resistance. These methods measure alcohol in the breath, not BAC itself, and other substances can sometimes interfere with a reading. Sources: [McGill University](https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/article/did-you-know/did-you-know-breathalyzers-dont-directly-measure-your-blood-alcohol-concentration); [Medical News Today](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/breathalyzer-test)."
}

{
  "status": "success",
  "user_request": "I mention breathalyzers in my essay at https://docs.google.com/document/d/1U3EYa9gum02TSImK8Z8xwWnfu6jLFjFL7v9onqT42lw/export?format=txt. What exactly is a breathalyzer and how does it work?",
  "response": "A breathalyzer is a device that detects ethanol—the alcohol in alcoholic drinks—in a person’s exhaled breath. As blood passes through the lungs, some ethanol passes into the air there and is breathed out. The device measures that breath alcohol and uses it to estimate blood alcohol concentration (BAC); it does not directly test the blood.\n\nMany devices use a fuel-cell sensor: ethanol reacts at electrodes inside the sensor, producing an electrical current. The device measures the current and converts it into an estimated breath-alcohol reading. Some instruments use infrared spectroscopy instead, measuring how much infrared light the breath sample absorbs at particular wavelengths. In either case, the reading depends on the instrument’s sensor and calibration. Recent alcohol in the mouth or poor calibration can affect results.\n\nFor your essay, a concise definition could be: “A breathalyzer is a device that measures alcohol in exhaled breath and uses the result to estimate blood alcohol concentration.” Sources retrieved: McGill University’s Office for Science and Society, https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/article/did-you-know/did-you-know-breathalyzers-dont-directly-measure-your-blood-alcohol-concentration; Smart Start, https://www.smartstartinc.com/blog/how-does-a-breathalyzer-work."
}

{
  "status": "success",
  "user_request": "I mention breathalyzers in my essay at https://docs.google.com/document/d/1U3EYa9gum02TSImK8Z8xwWnfu6jLFjFL7v9onqT42lw/export?format=txt. What exactly is a breathalyzer and how does it work?",
  "response": "A breathalyzer is a device that measures alcohol in a person’s breath and uses that measurement to estimate blood alcohol concentration (BAC). Alcohol in the bloodstream passes into the air in the lungs, so the device analyzes a breath sample—ideally a steady sample of air from deep in the lungs. The sensor detects alcohol and the device converts its measurement into an estimated reading. Common devices use a fuel-cell sensor, where alcohol produces an electrical current, or infrared spectroscopy, where the instrument measures how much infrared light the alcohol absorbs. Some simpler devices use semiconductor sensors whose electrical resistance changes when alcohol is present. A breathalyzer estimates BAC; it does not directly test a blood sample, and different sensor types can vary in how susceptible they are to interference."
}

